WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.170 --> 00:00:06.003
Today, we're going to
talk about using ares

00:00:06.003 --> 00:00:07.400
for course reserves.

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:09.390
Just to level set--
course reserves,

00:00:09.390 --> 00:00:11.810
if you're not familiar with
it, is course related material

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.230
that's made available, either
electronically or physically.

00:00:14.230 --> 00:00:15.813
And generally, it's
the library that's

00:00:15.813 --> 00:00:17.330
providing these services.

00:00:17.330 --> 00:00:20.180
And at Yale, we use
a system called Ares.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:22.730
It is the supported platform
for providing access

00:00:22.730 --> 00:00:25.540
to these course reserves.

00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.410
So just some brief information
about what the service is.

00:00:29.410 --> 00:00:31.630
So it's a commercial,
vendor-supported software

00:00:31.630 --> 00:00:34.650
package made by Atlas Systems.

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:37.230
At Yale, it launched
in the fall of 2013.

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:39.550
And it's been online since.

00:00:39.550 --> 00:00:41.990
And the service is
managed by the library

00:00:41.990 --> 00:00:45.350
and runs on university
ITS infrastructure.

00:00:45.350 --> 00:00:48.580
So just talking about how
it's being used currently

00:00:48.580 --> 00:00:53.200
across the university, Yale
College Arts and Sciences,

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:55.460
the graduate school,
and school management

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:57.660
are the key users
of this system.

00:00:57.660 --> 00:01:00.890
Medical and law schools
haven't used it yet.

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:02.630
Just to give you a
sense of how it's

00:01:02.630 --> 00:01:05.600
being used in the
activity in the system,

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.900
roughly 500 courses for this
spring that are using it,

00:01:08.900 --> 00:01:11.220
about 10,000 reserve items.

00:01:11.220 --> 00:01:14.120
And then, when it comes
to electronic resources,

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:17.150
there have been roughly
52,000 downloads this spring.

00:01:17.150 --> 00:01:21.470
So it's a fairly
actively used system.

00:01:21.470 --> 00:01:23.690
If you're unfamiliar
with Ares, just

00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:27.140
to talk about the
various roles that users

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:29.750
play within the
system, so faculty

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:33.130
or their designated users
place things on a reserve.

00:01:33.130 --> 00:01:35.690
And it's the library staff
that then takes those requests

00:01:35.690 --> 00:01:38.397
and processes them and
makes them available.

00:01:38.397 --> 00:01:40.480
And then, the students
view that reserve material.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:46.810
I don't know if
you've encountered

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:49.470
other systems in the
past, if you've used Ares.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.810
But in case you're
curious, benefits of Ares,

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.520
I think the biggest
thing, it's really

00:01:54.520 --> 00:01:56.110
tightly integrated with Canvas.

00:01:56.110 --> 00:01:57.400
So that's why we're here.

00:01:57.400 --> 00:02:02.350
You gain access to it, using one
credential, one place to visit.

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:04.900
And you gain access
to that user interface

00:02:04.900 --> 00:02:07.150
through Canvas, which is huge.

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.450
So instructors can place
reserve requests online.

00:02:10.450 --> 00:02:12.659
You don't need to
come to the library

00:02:12.659 --> 00:02:14.950
to do it anymore, to take a
book and put it on request.

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.105
That's all automated.

00:02:17.105 --> 00:02:17.980
This is nice feature.

00:02:17.980 --> 00:02:21.307
Instructors can arrange
and tag reserve items

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:22.390
to organize their courses.

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:25.680
So if you put 30 items on
reserve, you have a 15,

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.860
16 week semester, you
could tag it in such a way

00:02:29.860 --> 00:02:31.840
that your students
know exactly when

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.580
to interact with the material.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.107
And I'll show you an
example of them in a minute.

00:02:36.107 --> 00:02:38.690
This is a big one, I think, for
the library and the university

00:02:38.690 --> 00:02:40.550
in terms of risk.

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.350
There's a lot of
issues swirling around

00:02:42.350 --> 00:02:45.470
about copyright and copyright
clearance for reserved items.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.320
And the Ares systems
has a very nice workflow

00:02:48.320 --> 00:02:50.660
that the library staff use
to validate that things

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:54.710
are, in fact, appropriate to use
in the class for your students.

00:02:54.710 --> 00:02:57.710
And we will pay the
publishers whatever

00:02:57.710 --> 00:03:00.800
fees are associated with
disseminating that information.

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:02.860
So you know that, if
you're using the system,

00:03:02.860 --> 00:03:06.063
the material is OK to use.

00:03:06.063 --> 00:03:08.187
And you're not going to
get a call from any lawyer.

00:03:08.187 --> 00:03:09.650
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:09.650 --> 00:03:11.540
Finally, it's a nice
place to go and check

00:03:11.540 --> 00:03:13.730
accuracy of your reserve lists.

00:03:13.730 --> 00:03:16.580
And much like Panopto,
you can get a quick sense

00:03:16.580 --> 00:03:20.690
of what the statistical
usage is of the various items

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:22.780
on reserve.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.780
Benefits for students--
a couple of nice things.

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:27.320
Again, tightly
integrated with Canvas.

00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:30.300
They only have one place to
go to look at this stuff.

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:32.580
They have access to their
print reserve collections

00:03:32.580 --> 00:03:35.140
and e-reserves
collection in one place.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.060
So that's books that are put on
a shelf here in this library,

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.090
or it's links out to web sites,
or PDFs, or other materials,

00:03:42.090 --> 00:03:43.650
all in one place.

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:47.370
For students, this seems like
it's a fairly popular service.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:50.730
They can enable the system to
alert them when new items go

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:52.284
on reserve for a class.

00:03:52.284 --> 00:03:53.700
So they get immediate
notification

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:56.490
that, hey, this professor
put this book on reserve.

00:03:56.490 --> 00:03:59.318
They can also set it up in such
a way that it tells them, hey,

00:03:59.318 --> 00:04:01.109
I'm going to set up a
reminder to remind me

00:04:01.109 --> 00:04:04.839
that, in two weeks, I need to
look at this particular item.

00:04:04.839 --> 00:04:10.390
And finally, this
solution is a document--

00:04:10.390 --> 00:04:12.119
it's a place to
host the material.

00:04:12.119 --> 00:04:14.800
So if you upload PDFs,
they reside in Ares.

00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:17.349
So students can go and log
in, interact with the content,

00:04:17.349 --> 00:04:18.880
or download it for later use.

00:04:18.880 --> 00:04:21.579
So it's also a
publishing platform.

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:25.470
This is just one example
of a course in Canvas.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.460
And you'll notice
here, circled in red,

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:30.070
is where course reserves
will pop up, I believe,

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:32.960
when you actually
populate that list.

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:34.460
And then, this is
the student view.

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:39.026
If you actually click through to
course reserves, you can see--

00:04:39.026 --> 00:04:41.280
I don't know how big it is--

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:43.470
but on the bottom
part of the screen

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:45.210
are the actual items
that are on reserve.

00:04:45.210 --> 00:04:47.310
In this case, it's
physical material

00:04:47.310 --> 00:04:49.650
that, if they were to
actually click through

00:04:49.650 --> 00:04:51.782
to any of those items, it
would probably say, oh,

00:04:51.782 --> 00:04:52.740
go to the best library.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.280
Go to CSSSI up the
hill, or wherever.

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:59.640
If it's web pages or web
resources, it's just links out.

00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.960
If it's PDFs, you can
download the thing there.

00:05:01.960 --> 00:05:04.770
Thank you.

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:07.331
So going to hand it
off to Mary Beth.

00:05:07.331 --> 00:05:07.830
OK.

00:05:07.830 --> 00:05:09.570
Hi.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:11.550
I'm up at the hill at CSSSI.

00:05:11.550 --> 00:05:14.280
But all the units who do
reserves and put things

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:18.120
on reserve for you work
together as a group.

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:20.790
We refer things out
to other libraries.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:22.970
I just have a few
frequently asked questions

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:26.100
that I thought I would mention.

00:05:26.100 --> 00:05:28.680
Do I have to provide the
material to the library?

00:05:28.680 --> 00:05:30.300
No.

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:33.760
You can upload what you like.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.380
We'll be looking at copyright.

00:05:35.380 --> 00:05:40.410
So automatically, the default
is to check for copyright.

00:05:40.410 --> 00:05:43.270
But you can tell
us, get it for us,

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:46.540
and we'll get the article
for you, or the book.

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:47.980
Sometimes, we don't
have the book.

00:05:47.980 --> 00:05:49.690
We'll order the book.

00:05:49.690 --> 00:05:52.180
We'll look at how many
students are in the class.

00:05:52.180 --> 00:05:54.670
So if you know how many
students are in the class,

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:58.300
you can put that information
into the course record.

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:01.090
That will help us in figuring
out how many copies of a book

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:04.180
we need to have.

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:06.910
Do I need to tag
each reserve item?

00:06:06.910 --> 00:06:12.550
It's a really good idea
to tag each reserve item.

00:06:12.550 --> 00:06:15.030
Otherwise, it's hard for
people to find things.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:18.000
We get frantic calls,
usually Sunday night,

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:20.730
my professor said there's an
article supposed to be here,

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.800
and it's not here.

00:06:22.800 --> 00:06:25.350
Often, it's because they don't
know they can sort by tags,

00:06:25.350 --> 00:06:27.270
or because it wasn't tagged.

00:06:27.270 --> 00:06:29.550
And so they're having
trouble finding it.

00:06:29.550 --> 00:06:32.190
And we usually calm them
down and help them find it.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:33.630
What's a tag?

00:06:33.630 --> 00:06:37.800
So if you say, this is
for the first class,

00:06:37.800 --> 00:06:38.680
or the first week--

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:39.180
Oh, by time.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.070
By time or date.

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:45.540
It's probably preferable
to do by class, in a way,

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:48.720
because if you reuse
a list, the next year,

00:06:48.720 --> 00:06:50.680
the dates will be different.

00:06:50.680 --> 00:06:54.690
So if you use the first
class, second class,

00:06:54.690 --> 00:06:55.710
that kind of thing--

00:06:55.710 --> 00:07:02.510
and for 0 to 9, if
you use 0-9, 0-1, 0-2,

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.240
0-3, so that it
interfile correctly.

00:07:06.240 --> 00:07:07.479
It's fussy like that.

00:07:07.479 --> 00:07:09.520
But that's part of the
few fussy things about it.

00:07:12.487 --> 00:07:14.320
And you can tag them
with whatever you want.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.220
If you want to use
topic, instead of time--

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:18.750
so this is the
topic we're talking

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:20.370
about for the next three weeks.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:22.815
You can tag them
with time and topic.

00:07:25.350 --> 00:07:26.070
Next question.

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:28.440
What is a pickup location?

00:07:28.440 --> 00:07:30.630
We fixed it so it's easier.

00:07:30.630 --> 00:07:34.627
There used to be a
default of Bass Library--

00:07:34.627 --> 00:07:36.210
so if you didn't
catch that you needed

00:07:36.210 --> 00:07:41.130
to choose a pickup location,
or your request for processing

00:07:41.130 --> 00:07:42.570
went to Bass.

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:46.230
And since they're the largest
provider of reserves on campus,

00:07:46.230 --> 00:07:50.220
that could have put you
back farther in the queue.

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:54.960
Pickup location is where you
would like print materials

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:56.610
to be for your students.

00:07:56.610 --> 00:08:02.370
And that varies, depending
on what you're teaching.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:05.610
CSSSI is the sciences, but
the engineering collection

00:08:05.610 --> 00:08:06.970
is down here.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:12.730
And so Bass provides reserves
processing for engineering.

00:08:12.730 --> 00:08:13.230
Sorry.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:14.070
[INAUDIBLE] time.

00:08:14.070 --> 00:08:15.450
We need to take questions now.

00:08:15.450 --> 00:08:17.300
OK.

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:17.870
So thank you.

00:08:17.870 --> 00:08:18.943
Any questions?

00:08:18.943 --> 00:08:20.297
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:20.297 --> 00:08:22.297
How much of a book will
the library [INAUDIBLE].

00:08:22.297 --> 00:08:22.797
[LAUGHTER]

00:08:22.797 --> 00:08:25.500
I was hoping someone
would do that.

00:08:25.500 --> 00:08:30.840
So we have a guideline
that we will do 20%.

00:08:34.190 --> 00:08:38.660
It's a guideline, so it's a
little soft around the edges.

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:41.990
But 20% of a book.

00:08:41.990 --> 00:08:45.260
If the book is out
of copyright, and we

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:49.920
have the time and the
staffing, we can do much more.

00:08:49.920 --> 00:08:50.540
But it's 20%.

00:08:57.512 --> 00:08:59.006
OK.

00:08:59.006 --> 00:09:00.373
Thank you.

00:09:00.373 --> 00:09:00.998
You're welcome.

00:09:00.998 --> 00:09:01.994
Thank you.

00:09:01.994 --> 00:09:04.982
[APPLAUSE]

